# Backend build context exclusions.
#
# This file — not the one at the repository root — is what governs `context: ./backend`.
# Docker only reads the `.dockerignore` at the root of the build context, so without this the
# builder would upload the developer's Windows `.venv` (hundreds of MB of Linux-incompatible
# binaries), every cache directory, and any local `.env` straight into the image.

# ---- Virtualenvs (host-built, wrong platform, never wanted in the image) ----
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ---- Python caches / build artefacts ----
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.eggs/

# ---- Test & lint caches ----
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# ---- Local runtime state ----
# `var/` holds locally written media/exports; production mounts a volume instead.
var/
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.log

# ---- Secrets ----
# Never bake an environment file into an image. The example is kept out too: it is documentation.
.env
.env.*

# ---- Tests ----
# The production image runs the application, not the suite. CI installs the `dev` extra and runs
# tests from the source tree; shipping them only widens the image and its attack surface.
tests/

# ---- VCS / editor ----
.git/
.gitignore
.idea/
.vscode/
